        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1344881824,
        "heading": "Uses",
        "text": "Ephedra nevadensis was used for food and medicine by Native Americans and for tea by Mormons.  Currently, its biggest use is as forage for sheep and cattle (it is not as good for horses), and for habitat restoration.  It is sometimes grown for ornamental purposes. Among the Zuni people an infusion of the whole plant, except for the root, is taken for syphilis. They also use it to make a tea-like beverage.",
        "truncated": false
      }
